00:08:55.477 --> 00:08:57.810
VIKAS SAINI: All right, so
you heard from Ron on the use

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.190
case and the pain points.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:04.440
Now, let's discuss a solution
that was built to solve this.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.350
So this solution
is built leveraging

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:09.620
some of the technology from
our retail common services

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.330
platform.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:13.420
So before showing you
the actual architecture,

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.640
I'll quickly go over
some of the design

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:18.150
principles for this solution.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:21.330
One of the key architecture
principle of the solution

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:24.820
is that it's completely
event-based architecture.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:28.720
So most of the traditional
architectures are batch-based.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.180
All of the upstream
events are actually

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:34.400
written to the central
event's source.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:36.340
So as we would expect,
the event source

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.480
has to be highly available.

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.550
This event source
then publishes events

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:45.150
for other downstream
systems to use.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.740
You're also able to filter the
events into a central system

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:50.950
for fast querying from other
applications that need access

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:53.560
to this data.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:56.350
In parallel, you can
also load the events

00:09:56.350 --> 00:10:01.190
from the system for analytics
and learning and analysis.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:04.170
So if you want to give access
to the data for your data

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.660
engineering team or your data
science team to build models,

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:10.320
to do any sort of
analysis or recording,

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.520
they're able to do that without
affecting the core system.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:17.390
And lastly is that you can
also play back the events

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:19.590
from the archive if needed.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.380
So if you ever need
to go back and look

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:25.210
at the chronological order
of events, how they occurred,

00:10:25.210 --> 00:10:28.470
you're able to do
that with this system.

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:30.510
So let's look at
the architecture.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.630
So this slide shows the
architecture for the solution.

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:36.055
So as you'll see, there's
a lot of detail here.

00:10:36.055 --> 00:10:37.680
I'll go over some of
the key components

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.280
at a very high level.

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.740
The first step is
price optimization.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.100
So this is where we
classify price for items,

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:48.700
such as base price, markdown
price, discounts, er cetera.

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:51.720
So as you would expect,
every item or every SKU

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:55.380
needs to have a price
optimization process.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.480
After the price
optimization, the next step

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.870
is data transformation,
so ETL, if you will.

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:04.540
So we use Dataflow for
data transformation.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:08.500
And we also have a parser to
format and parse the data.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:12.660
Once the data is transformed, it
is ingested into Cloud Spanner.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.648
So Cloud Spanner has the
benefit of True Time.

00:11:15.648 --> 00:11:17.190
And what that means
is that it easily

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:19.860
lets you add a
globally-consistent timestamp

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:21.460
for all of your events.

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:23.130
So the benefit for
the solution is

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:26.850
that this establishes a ground
truth of the order of all

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.460
of your messages for all time.

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:30.960
This means that you can make
downstream assumptions anchored

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.820
on that ground
truth, which solves

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:36.570
a lot of complexity for
dependent systems and services.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:39.270
Up next are the
event-based triggers

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:41.250
that pull this data
into the messaging

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:43.830
queue from Cloud Spanner.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:46.747
And our messaging
queue is Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:11:46.747 --> 00:11:48.330
So this is the layer
where the records

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:51.660
are filtered and written
to a centralized messaging

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.490
queue pops up.

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:55.860
And the benefit of this is
that the multiple subscribers

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.130
can be registered to get data
from this messaging publishing

00:11:59.130 --> 00:11:59.850
queue.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:03.840
One of the main subscribers
is the master publish view.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.150
The data or the
events are loaded

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.550
to a corresponding
low-latency database

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:12.300
or materialized view
for easy query access.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:15.120
This has to be a really
low-latency database,

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.910
so think of it like an
in-memory database or a really

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.860
low latency NoSQL database.

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:23.290
It just depends on the
size of the database.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.920
We've actually tested
this pattern for millions

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.990
of records and
millions of products

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:29.652
and this holds pretty well.

00:12:29.652 --> 00:12:31.860
And one of the main reasons
why we're able to do that

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:34.320
is because we are
able to separate

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:36.750
the writes from the reads.

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:40.180
In parallel, we also
have the Archiver,

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:43.170
which writes these events to
a global archive called Cloud

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:45.360
Storage in a compressed state.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.330
And then corresponding to
the Archiver is a Replayer.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:49.980
So Replayer then
reads the records

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:52.230
in order of the
long-term storage

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:54.190
and then decompresses
them and loads them

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:55.440
into a new cloud upstream.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:58.740
So if you ever need to go back
and look how the events were

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:01.770
loaded into the system, how
you got certain data, what

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:04.320
was the chronological
order of all the events,

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:07.600
you're able to easily do
that with the Replayer.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:09.150
And lastly, we also
have the ability

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:14.620
to make one-off price
changes using the price UI.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:18.240
This price UI goes through
the centralized API

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.745
and writes information
into Cloud Spanner.

00:13:20.745 --> 00:13:22.620
And once the information
is in Cloud Spanner,

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:24.720
it goes through the
same process of pushing

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.630
the data to master publish
view and to the Archiver

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:29.320
or that we just discussed.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.140
So hopefully this
gives you an overview

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:34.040
of the solution that was
created to solve this use case.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:38.190
