00:17:30.188 --> 00:17:32.230
VIKAS SAINI: All right,
so as you heard from Ron,

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:35.020
this pattern provides
many important benefits

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:36.950
over traditional architecture.

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:40.490
The first one is
global consistency.

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:43.320
So Cloud Spanner has the
benefit of True Time.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:46.590
So it easily lets you add a
globally consistent timestamp

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:49.360
for each of your events.

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:51.030
So this establishes
a ground truth

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:54.270
of the order for all of
your messages at all time.

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.970
And you can have your
downstream systems work

00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:59.310
with that assumption,
which is a huge benefit

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:03.490
and solves a lot of complexity
for those downstream systems.

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:06.600
The next one is
high availability.

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:08.840
So multi-region writes
are necessary for systems

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:11.510
that run in multiple
places, east or west,

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:13.430
disaster recovery.

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:17.460
But multi-region writes are
also great for sending events

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:20.760
from a single system
in the data center

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:26.340
to multiple systems that run
in the stores in this example.

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:30.750
Or vice versa-- so for things
like price, inventory tracking,

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:33.990
real-time sales metrics,
this is important to have

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:37.260
high availability
across the board.

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:38.760
And, as you mentioned
earlier, we've

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:41.670
actually tested this solution
for millions and millions

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:43.770
of products across channel.

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:46.170
And the solution
holds pretty well.

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:49.240
So if we have some
very stringent SLAs

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:52.800
and you want to make sure that
the performance doesn't degrade

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.960
when you are using millions
and millions of record,

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:58.790
then this solution is probably
the right one for you.

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:00.730
And then as Ron mentioned
earlier as well,

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:06.440
is this lets you focus
on the strategic tasks.

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:09.860
And the dev teams are
focused on building business

00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:12.920
logic or the business
value directly associated

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:15.260
with the use case and
not worry about some

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:18.680
of the operational tasks of
keeping the servers up, scaling

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:22.820
them up or down, because the
system takes care of that.

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:26.830
This results in quicker time
to market and much lower

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:29.400
total cost of ownership
for the solution.

